406 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (    Traditional property-search platforms primarily rely on centralized listing databases, keyword-based search filters, and manually curated property catalogs. While digital real estate portals have significantly improved accessibility, most existing systems still depend on broad filtering mechanisms that fail to provide personalized recommendations or meaningful categorization. Understanding these existing techniques and their limitations provides essential context for the development of the proposed Property Recommendation System.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
407 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (2.1 Centralized Real Estate Listing Platforms)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
408 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (       The most common approach in modern property-search systems is the centralized listing model. In this architecture, a single platform maintains a large database of properties uploaded by agents, developers, or individual sellers. Users search through this centralized repository using filters such as location, price, size, and property type. Popular real estate portals like 99acres, MagicBricks, Housing.com, and NoBroker follow this model, supporting millions of active property listings and users.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (        This architecture provides several advantages, including large-scale data availability, map-based visualization, and integration with agent networks. Centralized platforms also offer advanced features such as price trends, nearby amenities, loan assistance, and verified listings. For many consumers, these systems serve as the primary gateway to property discovery and market research.)
/Pg 16 0 R
>>
endobj
