514 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (    The architecture of the system is built around the principles of Object-Oriented Programming to ensure modularity, code reuse, and extensibility. The Property base class functions as the core building block of the system. It stores common attributes such as price, location, size, and description. Specialized property types inherit from this class and implement additional fields or behaviors specific to their category. For example, a Residential Property subclass may contain attributes like number of bedrooms or furnishing status, while a Commercial Property subclass may include suitability for office or retail use.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
515 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (     The Application Logic Layer acts as the bridge between the user interface and the underlying data models. It contains methods to load predefined property objects, filter data based on user input, and execute overridden display behaviors in the subclasses. This layer also supports the recommendation mechanism by retrieving and presenting category-wise property suggestions.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
516 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ( )
/Pg 28 0 R
>>
endobj
517 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (     Encapsulation is implemented throughout the architecture to restrict direct access to data attributes. All critical fields are declared private, and accessor methods \(getters and setters\) are used to interact with them. This ensures data integrity and allows standardized validation in future enhancements.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
518 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (      Polymorphism plays a critical role in the architecture by allowing each subclass to override methods such as displayDetails\(\). This enables different types of properties to present distinct information formats to the user, even when accessed through a base class reference. As a result, the system maintains flexibility and minimizes code duplication.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
519 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (     The use of Java Collections in the Data Representation Layer allows the system to manage multiple property objects efficiently. Property lists can be iterated, filtered, and extended using standard Java data structures. The architecture is intentionally designed to support future improvements.)
/Pg 28 0 R
>>
endobj
